The super keyword in Java is used to refer to the parent class (superclass). It allows a subclass to access members (variables, methods, and constructors) of its parent class.

Why is super used?

The super keyword is mainly used for three purposes:

Access the parent class variable
Call the parent class method
Call the parent class constructor

Why is super used?

The super keyword is mainly used for three purposes:

Access the parent class variable
Call the parent class method
Call the parent class constructor

Access the Parent Class Variable

If both parent and child classes have variables with the same name, use super to access the parent's variable.

class Animal {
    String color = "White";
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String color = "Black";

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Child color: " + color);
        System.out.println("Parent color: " + super.color);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.display();
    }
}

Output:

Child color: Black
Parent color: White

class Animal {
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

    void show() {
        sound();         // Child method
        super.sound();   // Parent method
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.show();
    }
}

output
Dog barks
Animal makes a sound

Call the Parent Class Constructor

Use super() to call the parent class constructor.

class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        super(); // Calls Animal constructor
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
    }
}

Output:

Animal constructor
Dog constructor
Calling a Parameterized Parent Constructor
class Animal {
    Animal(String name) {
        System.out.println("Animal: " + name);
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        super("Tommy");
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
    }
}

Output:

Animal: Tommy
